        alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
        border: { bottom: { style: BorderStyle.SINGLE, size: 6, color: GOLD, space: 4 } },
        children: [new TextRun({ text: "━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━", size: 24, color: GOLD, font: "Arial" })]
      }),
      spacer(),
      new Paragraph({
        spacing: { before: 120, after: 120 },
        alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
        children: [new TextRun({ text: "THEOLOGICAL FRAMEWORK AND DOCTRINAL FOUNDATION", bold: true, size: 32, color: DARK, font: "Arial" })]
      }),
      new Paragraph({
        spacing: { before: 80, after: 80 },
        alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
        children: [new TextRun({ text: "A Comprehensive Defense of the Sacred Work Mandate", italics: true, size: 26, color: GRAY, font: "Arial" })]
      }),
      spacer(),
      new Paragraph({
        spacing: { before: 80, after: 80 },
        alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
        children: [new TextRun({ text: "Established as a Faith-Based Organization", size: 22, color: GRAY, font: "Arial" })]
      }),
      new Paragraph({
        spacing: { before: 40, after: 40 },
        alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
        children: [new TextRun({ text: "Under 26 U.S.C. § 508(c)(1)(A)", size: 22, color: GRAY, font: "Arial" })]
      }),
      new Paragraph({
        spacing: { before: 40, after: 1440 },
        alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
        children: [new TextRun({ text: "Austin, Texas", size: 22, color: GRAY, font: "Arial" })]
      }),

      divider(),

      // PREAMBLE
      heading1("PREAMBLE: THE NATURE OF THIS DOCUMENT"),

      body("This document constitutes the foundational theological framework of The Thessalonian Mandate Institute, a Faith-Based Organization established pursuant to 26 U.S.C. § 508(c)(1)(A). It serves simultaneously as a statement of doctrinal conviction, a declaration of sacred mission, and an authoritative defense of the theological mandate upon which this organization is built."),

      body("This framework is not a legal argument. It is a theological confession. The authors of this document hold these positions not because they are convenient, but because they are true — true to the biblical text, true to the historical tradition of the Christian faith, and true to the urgent need of the present moment."),

      body("Challenges to the sincerity or validity of this organization's theological basis are anticipated and addressed herein in full. No challenge has been left unanswered. Every doctrinal position stated in this document is grounded in scripture, supported by theological tradition, and demonstrated in practical mission."),

      spacer(),
      divider(),

      // PART I
      heading1("PART I: THE SCRIPTURAL FOUNDATION"),

      heading2("1.1 The Primary Mandate — 2 Thessalonians 3:10"),

      body("The doctrinal center of this organization is the explicit apostolic command recorded in Paul's second letter to the church at Thessalonica:"),

      ...scripture(
        "2 Thessalonians 3:10 (NIV)",
        "For even when we were with you, we gave you this rule: 'The one who is unwilling to work shall not eat.'"
      ),

      body("This verse is not a suggestion. It is not a cultural observation. Paul frames it explicitly as a rule — a binding apostolic directive given in person and now committed to writing for permanence. The Greek word used is παραγγέλλω (parangellō), the same term used for military orders. It carries the full weight of apostolic authority."),

      body("The implications of this command are radical: work is not optional for those who are able. It is a moral and spiritual obligation. An organization that equips people to fulfill this obligation is not merely performing a social service — it is performing a ministry of obedience to divine command."),

      heading2("1.2 The Creation Mandate — Genesis 2:15 and Genesis 3:19"),

      body("Paul's command in 2 Thessalonians does not originate with Paul. It reaches back to the first chapters of scripture:"),

      ...scripture(
        "Genesis 2:15 (NIV)",
        "The Lord God took the man and put him in the Garden of Eden to work it and take care of it."
      ),

      ...scripture(
        "Genesis 3:19 (NIV)",
        "By the sweat of your brow you will eat your food until you return to the ground, since from it you were taken; for dust you are and to dust you will return."
      ),

      body("Work precedes the Fall. In Genesis 2:15, God assigns Adam the task of tending and keeping the garden before sin enters the world. Work is therefore not punishment — it is the original vocation of humanity, established in the image and likeness of a God who himself worked in creation. The Fall in Genesis 3 does not eliminate the work mandate; it makes it harder. The sweat is new. The calling is not."),

      body("Paul understands this. His insistence on work in 2 Thessalonians is not a bourgeois imposition on the poor — it is a call back to the original dignity of human vocation. To deprive a person of the capacity to work is to deprive them of a fundamental dimension of their humanity. To restore that capacity is an act of profound theological significance."),

      heading2("1.3 Excellence in Craft as Virtue — Proverbs 22:29"),

      ...scripture(
        "Proverbs 22:29 (NIV)",
        "Do you see someone skilled in their work? They will serve before kings; they will not serve before officials of low rank."
      ),

      body("The wisdom tradition of Israel does not merely command work — it elevates skilled work as the pathway to honor, influence, and dignity. The Hebrew word for skilled (מָהִיר, māhîr) means swift, sharp, and ready — the kind of expertise that comes only through deliberate practice and genuine mastery."),

      body("This verse establishes a theological principle of direct relevance to our mission: the cultivation of technical skill is a sacred pursuit. A person who achieves mastery in a craft — including the technical crafts of the modern information economy — participates in a tradition of honorable labor that scripture explicitly commends. Our work of training people in technology is not secular vocational education dressed in religious language. It is the active cultivation of māhîr — skilled excellence — in obedience to the wisdom mandate of scripture."),

      heading2("1.4 Labor as Worship — Colossians 3:23"),

      ...scripture(
        "Colossians 3:23 (NIV)",
        "Whatever you do, work at it with all your heart, as working for the Lord, not for human masters."
      ),

      body("Paul's theology of work reaches its fullest expression in this verse. Every act of labor, performed with full devotion, becomes an act of worship. The secular and the sacred are not divided categories in Pauline theology — they are unified under the lordship of Christ. A displaced worker who learns to code and builds software for human flourishing is, in the fullest theological sense, working for the Lord."),

      body("This has a critical implication for our mission: we do not merely train people to get jobs. We restore people to the dignity of worshipful labor. Every curriculum module, every certification pathway, every hour of instruction is an act of ministry — because we are restoring human beings to their God-given vocation of skilled, purposeful, wholehearted work."),

      heading2("1.5 Preparation as Wisdom — Proverbs 24:27"),

      ...scripture(
        "Proverbs 24:27 (NIV)",
        "Put your outdoor work in order and get your fields ready; after that, build your house."
      ),

      body("The wisdom tradition commends preparation and the proper ordering of priorities. Build your capacity before you build your security. Develop your ability to produce before you establish your place of rest. This principle directly validates a ministry of workforce preparation — the deliberate work of equipping people with productive capacity before they can establish stability is not merely practical advice, it is biblical wisdom in action."),

      spacer(),
      divider(),

      // PART II
      heading1("PART II: THE HISTORICAL AND CONTEXTUAL FOUNDATION"),

      heading2("2.1 The Church at Thessalonica — Who Paul Was Writing To"),

      body("The letters to the Thessalonians were written to a specific community in a specific context, and that context is directly analogous to the moment we currently inhabit. Thessalonica was the capital of the Roman province of Macedonia — a major Mediterranean seaport, a hub of trade, commerce, and skilled labor. The city sat at the intersection of the Egnatian Way, one of the great Roman trade routes connecting east and west."),

      body("The church Paul founded there consisted largely of manual laborers, tradespeople, and working poor — people whose labor was the foundation of their dignity and their survival. Some among them had stopped working, apparently convinced that the imminent return of Christ made continued labor unnecessary. Paul's rebuke is sharp and specific: this is not faith, it is disorder. The Greek word he uses for their behavior — ἀτάκτως (ataktōs) — is a military term meaning out of formation, undisciplined, operating outside proper order."),

      body("The parallel to our moment is not metaphorical — it is structural. Technological displacement is removing people from productive labor not through theological confusion but through economic disruption. The result is the same: people who are willing to work but cannot. Our mission is to restore them to formation — to productive, dignified, skillful participation in the economy of their time."),

      heading2("2.2 Paul as a Working Minister — The Theological Precedent of Tent-Making"),

      body("Paul's command to work was not theoretical. He was himself a craftsman — a tent-maker by trade — and he deliberately practiced his craft even while engaged in full-time apostolic ministry. In the very same passage of 2 Thessalonians, he writes:"),

      ...scripture(
        "2 Thessalonians 3:7-9 (NIV)",
        "For you yourselves know how you ought to follow our example. We were not idle when we were with you, nor did we eat anyone's food without paying for it. On the contrary, we worked night and day, laboring and toiling so that we would not be a burden to any of you. We did this, not because we do not have the right to such help, but in order to offer ourselves as a model for you to imitate."
      ),

      body("Paul establishes himself as the model. He had the right to be supported by the church — he chose to work anyway, specifically so that his life would be a living argument for the theology he was preaching. Work, for Paul, was not a concession to economic necessity. It was a theological statement. An organization that follows this model — that does the work, that trains others to work, that embodies in its own operations the theology it proclaims — stands in direct apostolic succession to this tradition."),

      heading2("2.3 The Theological Tradition of the Work Mandate"),

      body("The theology of work embodied in this organization's mission is not a novelty. It stands within a long and distinguished tradition of Christian reflection on labor, vocation, and human dignity:"),

      body("Martin Luther recovered the doctrine of vocation (Beruf) from medieval distortion, arguing that the cobbler who makes good shoes for the glory of God serves God as fully as the monk who prays. Every legitimate occupation is a divine calling. The Reformation theology of vocation directly supports a ministry that equips people to pursue excellence in technical trades."),

      body("John Calvin extended Luther's insight, arguing that diligent labor in one's vocation is itself a form of service to God and neighbor. The Calvinist work ethic — which shaped the economic culture of the English-speaking world — is grounded in exactly the theology that drives our mission."),

      body("The Catholic social teaching tradition, from Pope Leo XIII's Rerum Novarum through the present day, insists on the dignity of labor and the right of workers to participate fully in the economic life of their society. Technological displacement that strips workers of meaningful labor is, in this tradition, a social justice issue with direct moral and theological dimensions."),

      body("Our mission does not represent a fringe theological position. It represents the mainstream of Christian reflection on work, labor, and human dignity across five centuries of tradition."),

      spacer(),
      divider(),

      // PART III
      heading1("PART III: THE DOCTRINAL POSITIONS OF THIS ORGANIZATION"),

      heading2("3.1 Statement of Faith"),

      body("The Thessalonian Mandate Institute holds the following theological convictions as foundational, non-negotiable, and sincerely held:"),

      heading3("On Scripture"),
      body("We believe the Holy Scriptures of the Old and New Testaments to be the inspired Word of God, authoritative in matters of faith and practice, and the ultimate standard against which all doctrine and action must be measured."),

      heading3("On the Nature of Work"),
      body("We believe that work is a divine institution, established by God before the Fall as the original vocation of humanity, and that the capacity for productive labor is a gift of God that reflects the image of the Creator in human beings. We believe that idleness — particularly willful idleness in those who are able to work — is a violation of the created order and a form of dishonor toward God."),

      heading3("On the Mandate to Work"),
      body("We believe that the apostolic command of 2 Thessalonians 3:10 constitutes a binding theological mandate: those who are able to work are obligated to work. We further believe that this mandate extends to the community of faith — that the church has a corresponding obligation to equip those who are willing to work but lack the capacity to do so. To witness a person's productive capacity destroyed by forces beyond their control and to do nothing is a violation of the spirit of this mandate."),

      heading3("On Technological Displacement as a Spiritual Crisis"),
      body("We believe that the mass displacement of workers by automation and artificial intelligence constitutes a spiritual crisis as well as an economic one. It is a crisis of vocation — a forcible removal of people from their God-given calling to productive labor. This crisis demands a theological response, not merely a policy response. The Church of Jesus Christ has both the obligation and the unique capacity to lead that response."),

      heading3("On Technical Education as Ministry"),
      body("We believe that the work of equipping displaced workers with the skills necessary to participate in the modern economy is a direct act of ministry — an application of the work mandate of scripture to the conditions of the present age. We do not merely provide job training. We restore human beings to the dignity of their God-given vocation. This is sacred work."),

      heading3("On Excellence"),
      body("We believe that the standard of Proverbs 22:29 — skilled excellence that commands respect and opens doors — is the appropriate standard for all instruction offered by this organization. We do not train people to barely qualify for entry-level positions. We train people to stand before kings."),

      spacer(),
      divider(),

      // PART IV
      heading1("PART IV: DEFENSE AGAINST ANTICIPATED CHALLENGES"),

      heading2("4.1 Challenge: This Organization Is a Pretext"),

      body("The anticipated challenge: that The Thessalonian Mandate Institute is not a genuinely religious organization but rather a secular workforce training program using religious language to obtain tax advantages."),

      body("The response: This challenge inverts the actual sequence. The theological conviction came first. The work mandate of 2 Thessalonians 3:10 is not a framing device applied to a pre-existing workforce training program. It is the origin and the foundation. The program exists because the theology demands it. An organization that believes, as a matter of sincere religious conviction, that displaced workers must be restored to productive vocation — and that acts on that belief by building a program to do so — is not a pretext. It is a ministry."),

      body("Furthermore, the 508(c)(1)(A) exemption exists precisely because the government has historically recognized that faith-based organizations operate from a different logic than secular nonprofits. The Thessalonian Mandate Institute operates from a theological logic: the work mandate is real, the crisis is real, the response is obligatory. Every aspect of this organization's structure follows from that logic."),

      heading2("4.2 Challenge: The Theological Basis Is Thin"),

      body("The anticipated challenge: that 2 Thessalonians 3:10 is being read out of context, or that the connection between that text and a modern workforce development program is a stretch."),

      body("The response: Part I and Part II of this document address this challenge comprehensively. The scriptural foundation of this organization rests not on a single verse read in isolation, but on a convergent body of testimony across both Testaments — from Genesis 2:15 through Proverbs 22:29 through Colossians 3:23 through 2 Thessalonians 3:10. These texts are not cherry-picked proof-texts. They constitute a coherent biblical theology of work that scholars across the theological spectrum recognize as central to Christian teaching."),

      body("The contextual reading of 2 Thessalonians 3:10, presented in Section 2.1, establishes that Paul was writing to a community of working people in a trade-centered city whose productive capacity was being disrupted by theological confusion. The structural parallel to technological displacement is not forced — it is the natural application of an enduring theological principle to a new set of material conditions. This is how Christian theology has always worked."),

      heading2("4.3 Challenge: Workforce Training Is Not Ministry"),

      body("The anticipated challenge: that job training is a secular activity and cannot constitute genuine religious ministry regardless of the theological framing applied to it."),

      body("The response: This challenge rests on a dualism between sacred and secular that the New Testament explicitly rejects. Colossians 3:23 — 'Whatever you do, work at it with all your heart, as working for the Lord' — demolishes the sacred-secular divide at its foundation. There is no category of legitimate human labor that falls outside the scope of this verse."),

      body("Moreover, the Reformation doctrine of vocation — articulated by Luther, developed by Calvin, and embedded in the theological DNA of the Protestant tradition — holds that every legitimate occupation is a divine calling. A ministry that equips people to pursue their calling is not performing a secular activity with religious decoration. It is performing the distinctly theological work of restoring human beings to their vocation."),

      body("Finally, the precedent of Paul himself as tent-maker — a working minister who practiced a skilled trade as a deliberate theological act — establishes beyond dispute that skilled technical work and genuine ministry are not merely compatible. They can be identical."),

      spacer(),
      divider(),

      // PART V
      heading1("PART V: THE CONTEMPORARY MISSION"),

      heading2("5.1 The Crisis of Technological Displacement"),

      body("The Industrial Revolution displaced agricultural workers. The automation of manufacturing displaced factory workers. The current revolution in artificial intelligence and digital technology is displacing knowledge workers, clerical workers, and service workers at a scale and speed that exceeds any previous technological transition in human history."),

      body("The people most affected are not the wealthy and educated, who can adapt most easily. They are the working class — people who built their lives around skills that technology has now made obsolete. Many of them are people of faith who understand their work as a calling, not merely a paycheck. They are not lazy. They are not unwilling. They are displaced. They need equipping."),

      heading2("5.2 The Response of The Thessalonian Mandate Institute"),

      body("The Thessalonian Mandate Institute exists to provide that equipping. Our program of instruction focuses on the technical fields with the highest demand and the lowest barrier to entry for career changers: information technology support, cybersecurity, cloud computing, data analysis, and AI-assisted workflow management."),

      body("These fields were selected not arbitrarily but according to the standard of Proverbs 22:29: they are fields where genuine skill commands genuine respect, where mastery opens real doors, and where a displaced worker who completes our program can credibly stand before kings — before hiring managers, before executives, before the economy itself — and offer something of real value."),

      body("Our method of instruction is cohort-based and community-rooted. Participants do not merely receive information — they are received into a community of people committed to the same theological conviction: that work is sacred, that skill is honorable, and that every person willing to work deserves the capacity to do so."),

      heading2("5.3 The Measure of Our Success"),

      body("We measure our success not by enrollment numbers or revenue, but by the standard scripture sets: do the people who complete our program have the skill to serve before kings? Are they equipped to fulfill the work mandate with excellence? Are they restored to the dignity of productive, purposeful, worshipful labor?"),

      body("This is the mission of The Thessalonian Mandate Institute. It is grounded in scripture, supported by tradition, demanded by the present moment, and pursued with the full conviction of sincere religious faith."),

      spacer(),
      divider(),

      // CONCLUSION
      heading1("CONCLUSION: THE MANDATE STANDS"),

      body("The theological foundation of The Thessalonian Mandate Institute is not constructed. It is confessed. We do not hold these beliefs because they are useful. We hold them because they are true, and because truth obligates a response."),

      body("The work mandate of 2 Thessalonians 3:10 has stood for two thousand years. It stood in the tent-making workshops of the Roman Empire. It stood in the guilds of medieval Europe. It stood in the factories of the Industrial Revolution. It stands now, in the age of artificial intelligence, with the same authority and the same demand: the one who is willing to work must be equipped to work."),

      body("The Thessalonian Mandate Institute exists to make that possible. We do this as an act of faith, as an act of ministry, and as an act of obedience to the sacred work mandate of scripture."),

      spacer(),

      new Paragraph({
        spacing: { before: 480, after: 120 },
        alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
        border: { top: { style: BorderStyle.SINGLE, size: 4, color: GOLD, space: 8 } },
        children: [new TextRun({ text: "THE THESSALONIAN MANDATE INSTITUTE", bold: true, size: 24, color: BLUE, font: "Arial" })]
      }),
      new Paragraph({
        spacing: { before: 0, after: 80 },
        alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
        children: [new TextRun({ text: "Austin, Texas", italics: true, size: 22, color: GRAY, font: "Arial" })]
      }),
      new Paragraph({
        spacing: { before: 0, after: 80 },
        alignment: AlignmentType.CENTER,
        children: [new TextRun({ text: "Established under 26 U.S.C. § 508(c)(1)(A)", size: 20, color: GRAY, font: "Arial" })]
      }),
    ]
  }]
});

Packer.toBuffer(doc).then(buffer => {
  fs.writeFileSync('/mnt/user-data/outputs/TMI_Theological_Framework.docx', buffer);
  console.log('Document written successfully');
}).catch(err => {
  console.error('Error:', err);
});

